## Limiting Access to cloud-init

The metadata-service gives each node its cloud-init configuration, including
the munge key, and its resource API (`groups`, `clusterdefaultss`,
`instanceinfos` and `wireguardpeers`) changes that configuration for every
node. Neither asks for a credential. haproxy publishes the service under
`/metadata-service` on ports 8081 and 8443 of the head node. The steps below
have haproxy answer `/metadata-service` only from privileged source ports
(1-1023), which only root can use, and its resource API only from the head node
itself. podman's published ports keep the client's address and source port, so
haproxy sees them as the client sent them.

Root on the compute nodes gets a privileged source port from the nftables rules
in the compute image (see [Building the Base OS
Image](#building-the-base-os-image)). Root on the head node gets one from the
rules below, so `ochami metadata` commands keep working when run as root.
`ochami` connects to `https://${sms_name}:8443`, so the rules cover every IPv4
address `${sms_name}` resolves to, as well as `${sms_ip}` (a container head, for
example, also maps the name to its container address); the first step below
collects them and stops if the name resolves to IPv6, which the rules don't
cover. They don't work as another user, or through `127.0.0.1`.

This is the same trust model as Warewulf's secure mode. It assumes ports below
1024 are root-only on the head and compute nodes, so both pin
`net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start` at 1024, and that root on any other host
on the provisioning network is trusted.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Keep ports below 1024 root-only on the head node -->
```bash
if getent ahosts "${sms_name}" | awk '{print $1}' | grep -q ':'; then
	echo "ERROR: ${sms_name} resolves to IPv6; use IPv4 only for it" >&2
	exit 1
fi
sms_head_addrs=$( (echo "${sms_ip}"; getent ahostsv4 "${sms_name}" \
	| awk '{print $1}') | sort -u | paste -sd, -)
echo "net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start = 1024" \
	> /etc/sysctl.d/90-ohpc-privports.conf
sysctl -q -p /etc/sysctl.d/90-ohpc-privports.conf
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

First add the access rules to haproxy's `openchami` frontend, next to the
`/metadata-service` path rule, check the configuration, and reload haproxy in
place. haproxy's master process re-reads its configuration on `SIGUSR2`.
Restarting `haproxy.service` instead also restarts the other services in
`openchami.target`. Right after start-up those services can still be
restarting, so the check and reload are retried until haproxy's container
stays up long enough for both.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Limit the metadata-service to privileged ports, its API to the head -->
```bash
haproxy_cfg=/etc/openchami/configs/haproxy.cfg
acl_file=/opt/ohpc/admin/haproxy/metadata-access.cfg
mkdir -p /opt/ohpc/admin/haproxy
cat > "${acl_file}" <<EOF
  # OpenHPC: /metadata-service only from privileged source ports, and
  # its resource API only from the head node
  acl SRC_privport src_port 1:1023
  acl SRC_head src ${sms_ip}
  acl PATH_metadata-api path_beg -i /metadata-service/groups
  acl PATH_metadata-api path_beg -i /metadata-service/clusterdefaultss
  acl PATH_metadata-api path_beg -i /metadata-service/instanceinfos
  acl PATH_metadata-api path_beg -i /metadata-service/wireguardpeers
  http-request deny deny_status 403 if PATH_metadata-service !SRC_privport
  http-request deny deny_status 403 if PATH_metadata-api !SRC_head
EOF
if ! grep -q 'acl SRC_privport' "${haproxy_cfg}"; then
	sed -i "/^ *acl PATH_metadata-service *path_beg/r ${acl_file}" "${haproxy_cfg}"
fi
grep -q 'acl SRC_privport' "${haproxy_cfg}" \
	|| { echo "ERROR: metadata access rules not in ${haproxy_cfg}" >&2; exit 1; }
for ((t=0; ; t++)); do
	podman exec haproxy haproxy -c -q -f /usr/local/etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg \
		&& podman kill --signal USR2 haproxy >/dev/null && break
	(( t < 12 )) || { echo "haproxy: reload FAILED"; exit 1; }
	echo "Waiting for haproxy..."
	sleep 5
done
echo "haproxy: reloaded"
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Then the head node's own rules. Root's connections to ports 8081 and 8443 on
`${sms_ip}` leave from `${sms_ip}` with a source port between 600 and 1023.
Other users can't reach the OpenCHAMI container networks directly, which would
bypass haproxy. The networks' subnets are read from podman. A dedicated unit
loads the rules at boot, not `nftables.service`: stopping or reloading that
service flushes the whole ruleset, podman's own rules included.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Head node source-port rules for the metadata-service -->
```bash
podman_nets=$(podman network inspect \
	--format '{%raw%}{{range .Subnets}}{{.Subnet}} {{end}}{%endraw%}' \
	$(podman network ls --format '{%raw%}{{.Name}}{%endraw%}' | grep '^openchami-') \
	| tr ' ' '\n' | grep '^[0-9.]*/[0-9]*$' | paste -sd, -)
if [[ -z "${podman_nets}" ]]; then
	echo "ERROR: no OpenCHAMI podman network subnets found" >&2
	exit 1
fi
cat > /etc/nftables/ohpc-sport.nft <<EOF
table inet ohpc_sport
delete table inet ohpc_sport
table inet ohpc_sport {
	chain output {
		type filter hook output priority mangle; policy accept;
		ip daddr { ${sms_head_addrs} } tcp dport { 8081, 8443 } meta skuid 0 \\
			meta mark set 0x421
		ip daddr { ${podman_nets} } meta skuid != 0 \\
			reject with icmpx admin-prohibited
	}
	chain postrouting {
		type nat hook postrouting priority srcnat; policy accept;
		meta mark 0x421 meta l4proto tcp snat ip to ${sms_ip}:600-1023
	}
}
EOF
cat > /etc/systemd/system/ohpc-sport.service <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=OpenHPC source-port rules for the metadata-service
After=nftables.service
Before=network-pre.target haproxy.service
Wants=network-pre.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
ExecStart=/usr/sbin/nft -f /etc/nftables/ohpc-sport.nft
ExecStop=/usr/sbin/nft delete table inet ohpc_sport

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable --now ohpc-sport.service
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->
